From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: i2c: Add a driver for the Samsung S5K3L6 sensor

The S5K3L6 is a 13 MP raw sensor with a MIPI CCS register map. Some
Huawei MateBook E Go units carry it as their rear camera (Lijing module)
instead of the OmniVision OV13B10.

The power sequence, the init settings and the four modes are those of
the module configuration that the Windows camera driver ships for that
module, at a 24 MHz MCLK: 4160x3120, 4160x2336 cropped and 2080x1560
binned at 30 fps, and 1920x1080 binned at 60 fps, from a 480 MHz pixel
clock over four lanes at 600, 284 or 260 MHz depending on the mode. The
Librem5 s5k3l6xx driver's settings need 25 MHz and give 8 fps at full
size. As in the Windows settings, the sensor corrects its phase
detection pixels itself (bit 2 of register 0x3403).

No mode reads out more than the centred 4160 columns of the 4208 pixel
wide active array, which the driver reports as the default crop
rectangle. The exposure stays 8 lines below the frame length, as in the
Windows configuration. The Bayer order, the analogue gain range and the
minimum exposure come from the sensor's CCS registers at probe.

Each mode's default frame length is that of the Windows settings: 3260
lines (30 fps), and 1630 lines (60 fps) for the 1080p mode. Both binned
modes read a line in the same time, so the 2080x1560 mode's 3260 lines
are mostly vertical blanking: its frame length may go down to 1634
lines, 60 fps at the full field of view. The other modes keep the
Windows frame length as their shortest. The sensor's CCS minimum frame
blanking, 25 lines, is not usable: the sensor stops its output, until
streaming restarts, with 25 lines of vertical blanking in the binned
modes and with 40 in the modes 4160 columns wide, while 32 and 50 lines
still work.

Tested on an S5K3L6 unit over the camss RDI: identification, the Bayer
order of each flip combination (colour bar test pattern and green
sites), linear exposure and analogue gain over a black level of 64,
the frame length limits above, 4160x3120 at 30 fps and 2080x1560 at
60 fps with 1634 lines; and through the camera HAL, 4160x3120 at 30
fps on its own, 4160x3120 photos with the display output, and a 1080p
stream and recording at 60 fps.

